{
  "decline_reason": "",
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Start with the statutory test: the resemblance must be such that it is likely to deceive or cause confusion; visual similarity alone is not the stated test.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMA1995/s10",
          "quote": "For the purposes of this Act, a trade mark is taken to be deceptively similar to another trade mark if it so nearly resembles that other trade mark that it is likely to deceive or cause confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "legislation"
    },
    {
      "text": "Under the Manual, assess how the similarities and differences between the marks as wholes affect an ordinary consumer’s impression or recollection, and whether that creates a real and tangible danger of confusion.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "In particular, in evaluating the likelihood of confusion, the marks must be judged as a whole"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/4~1",
          "quote": "What needs to be assessed is the effect of the similarities and differences on the impression or recollection an ordinary consumer of the goods or services would have of the trade marks and whether this creates a real and tangible danger of confusion."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "For a word mark versus a pictorial device, specifically compare the name and idea consumers would associate with the device with the name and idea conveyed by the word.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/12",
          "quote": "The examiner needs to consider the name and idea that would be associated with the particular device trade mark compared to the name and idea conveyed by the word trade mark."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Different forms of representation do not prevent deceptive similarity where a person of normal intelligence and memory would carry away the same overall impression.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/12",
          "quote": "A word and an equivalent pictorial representation may be found to be deceptively similar, based upon the fact that a person of normal intelligence and memory would retain and carry away the same overall impression."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Check how specifically the device conveys the word’s idea: the Manual contrasts an EMU device conveying precisely the name and idea EMU with shark depictions that did not establish the more specific idea of Great White Sharks.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/12",
          "quote": "the only possible conclusion in the present opposition is that the idea of the applicant’s EMU device is “EMU”, precisely the same idea and name that would be associated with the opponent’s trade mark for the word EMU."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/12",
          "quote": "A contrary outcome can be seen in Wolter Joose on behalf of Joose Apparel Pty Ltd v Great White Shark Enterprises Inc [1999] ATMO 59 where the following trade marks were found NOT to be deceptively similar:"
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/12",
          "quote": "whilst the ‘devices are definitely depictions of sharks…there is nothing to say that they are Great White Sharks’."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "If the device mark is a composite containing words, do not too readily treat those words as its essential feature and effectively disregard the device.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~1",
          "quote": "some caution needs to be exercised before characterising words in a complex composite registered trade mark as an ‘essential feature’ of that mark in assessing the question of deceptive similarity. If such a characterisation is made too readily, it effectively converts a composite mark into something quite different."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Give descriptive wording appropriate reduced weight, but do not ignore it entirely.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~1",
          "quote": "The correct approach, as noted above, is for decision makers to proceed on the basis that descriptive words may to some extent be discounted, but must not be ignored entirely."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Consider whether the device’s placement changes the overall impression by making particular wording dominant or sidelining other wording.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~3",
          "quote": "the placement of the device in the middle of the [realEstate1] logo is a strongly distinguishing feature. That placement has the effect of sidelining “.com.au” and projecting “realEstate1’ as the dominant element"
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    },
    {
      "text": "Assess visual differences through imperfect recollection rather than assuming consumers will remember fine details of a device or the arrangement of lettering.",
      "quotes": [
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~2",
          "quote": "there is very little likelihood that the relevant consumer would recall in any detail the look of the flame."
        },
        {
          "ref": "TMM/Part26/6/6/8~2",
          "quote": "But through the lens of imperfect recollection they are visually, aurally and conceptually indistinguishable from the Trade Mark—the relevant consumer cannot be expected to note, let alone recall, that the Opponent uses a superscript ‘The’ and the Applicant a lowercase ‘store’."
        }
      ],
      "authority": "manual"
    }
  ],
  "declined": false,
